<h1>Dawn: User Guide</h1>

<p class="details">
<span id="author">The Dawn Authors</span><br>
<span id="revnumber">version @PROJECT_VERSION@, 2026-10-01</span>

<h2>Introduction</h2>

<p><i>Dawn</i> is a colour-managed image browser and viewer.

<p>Its main purpose is to show images in the way they were meant to be shown,
and to let you easily navigate collections of them.  It does this with
a friendly interface that works across all major operating systems.

<h2>Overview</h2>

<p>The application has two main views: the browser and the viewer, pictured here
in the background and foreground respectively:

<p><img src=dn.webp width=1113 height=983 alt="Screenshot of Dawn" />

<h2>Basic Operation</h2>

<p>To quickly orient yourself, browse the application menu, or follow toolbar
tooltips.  The macOS build uses the standard menu bar.  On other systems,
the menu is shown by clicking the leftmost toolbar button (so-called hamburger),
or by pressing either the <kbd>Alt</kbd> or the <kbd>F10</kbd> key.
(Context menus are shown on right click, or by pressing either <kbd>Menu</kbd>
or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F10</kbd>.)

<p>For keyboard-only use, either use <kbd>Tab</kbd>/<kbd>F6</kbd>-based
navigation, or the Vimperator/Vimium-inspired hints invokable by
the <kbd>F</kbd> key: type the name of the chip you want to activate.

<p>Mouse is not fully documented within the application itself, and therefore
described below.

<h3>Browser</h3>

<ul>
<li><i>Middle click:</i> open items in new windows.
</ul>

<h3>Viewer</h3>

<ul>
<li><i>Left or middle button drag:</i> pan the view.
<li><i>Scroll wheel:</i> zoom in or out.
<li><i><kbd>Control</kbd>/<kbd>Command</kbd>+middle button drag:</i>
smooth zoom change.
<li><i><kbd>Alt</kbd>/<kbd>Option</kbd>+middle button drag:</i>
with an unlocked view, rotate the image arbitrarily.
</ul>

<h2>Browser Mode</h2>

<p>The browser is specialised for images.  The path to the current directory,
and all direct subdirectories are shown in the left sidebar.  The upper part
of it additionally shows recognised drives, your home directory,
your <q>pictures</q> directory, and any bookmarks you add using context menus.

<p>The main view only shows image files, as recognised by their file extension.
Turn off <i>Hide Unsupported Files</i> if this file filtering is too strict.

<h2>Viewer Mode</h2>

<p>The viewer has a few unusual features that deserve commentary:</p>

<dl>
<dt><i>Colour management</i> toggle
<dd><p>When disabled, <i>Dawn</i> will try to get pixel data on your screen
directly, bypassing colour management.  This is not always possible,
for example in HDR.
<dt><i>Process in Encoded Space</i> toggle
<dd><p>This toggles gamma-correct processing, which is normally off for
transparent images, where the rendering difference is more pronounced.
Images do not typically assume the correct rendition.
<dt><i>Browser-like Animation Delays</i> toggle
<dd><p>Web browsers typically push all frames shorter than 11 milliseconds
to 100 milliseconds.  Some animated images play way too fast when this option
is disabled.  Some may play too slow if enabled.
<dt>Information sidebar: <i>Enable JPEG Quant Smooth</i>
<dd><p>This enables a computation-intensive plugin that smooths out JPEG files
on load, greatly reducing DCT artefacts.
</dl>

<h2>System Configuration</h2>

<p>This section addresses three features of modern monitors that you can
exploit: non-sRGB gamuts, 10-bit colour depth, and high dynamic range (HDR).

<p>Your system generally does not know the effective gamut of your monitor,
so you must assign it manually, such as by associating the monitor with an ICC
profile.  Popular colour profile targets (Adobe RGB, Display P3) are either
already stored somewhere on your system (varies), or they can be downloaded off
the Internet.  For sub-sRGB laptop screens, check your manufacturer's download
page.  Windows may also pull these in automatically.

<p>10-bit colour and HDR need support from your monitor, your display cable,
your GPU, and your operating system.

<p>For HDR, your monitor may need to be put in a specific mode, typically PQ
over BT.2020.  The gamut situation is reversed here: processing is typically
done by the monitor.  Therefore, if you only plan to run HDR, you may ignore
a lot of the notes here.

<h3>macOS</h3>
<p>You're probably all set already.  Make sure your <i>Displays</i> in <i>System
Settings</i> have the right <i>Colour profile</i> assigned.  Apple uses
<i>Display P3</i> for its own devices.

<p>As a side note, you may want to use the <i>Better Display</i> application to
prevent macOS from using temporal dithering, so that it actually sends 10 bits
to the display.

<h3>Windows</h3>
<p>You're probably all set already.  Make sure your displays in
<i>colorcpl.exe</i> have the right colour profile assigned.  HDR needs to be
turned on in <i>Settings</i> manually.

<h3>Linux/BSD</h3>
<p>If you use KDE/Plasma/KWin on Wayland, assign the colour profile in its
<i>Display Configuration</i>.

<p>Otherwise set up your displays in <i>colord</i>, either through GUIs,
or through <i>colormgr</i>.  This step is crucial for GNOME (Mutter):

<pre>
$ colormgr get-profiles --filter 'title,profile-id'
title:Compatible with Adobe RGB (1998)
profile-id:icc-530ed29434cce1d2894fa84f54f47056
...
$ colormgr get-devices-by-kind display
$ colormgr device-add-profile xrandr-Eizo-CS2731-00000000 icc-530ed29434cce1d2894fa84f54f47056
$ colormgr device-make-profile-default xrandr-Eizo-CS2731-00000000 icc-530ed29434cce1d2894fa84f54f47056
</pre>

<p>As a last resort, you can enforce an ICC profile in <i>Dawn</i>'s
own settings.

<h3>Linux/BSD + X11</h3>
<p>The default on X11 is <q>everything undershoots/overshoots sRGB, unless it
does its own colour management</q>.  Be mindful of this limitation when
selecting a different gamut on your monitor.  Web browsers should be okay,
though <i>Firefox</i> may need to have <i>gfx.color_management.mode</i>
set to 1.

<p>If <code>colormgr get-devices-by-kind display</code> returns nothing within
your session, you may need to set up <i>colord-kde</i> or <i>xiccd</i> to
integrate <i>colord</i> into it.  If your setup is based on something other than
<i>colord</i>&mdash;notably ArgyllCMS/dispwin&mdash;<i>Dawn</i> also tries to
look up <i>_ICC_PROFILE</i>.

<p>While you may get 10-bit colour depth, many applications, such as <i>peek</i>
or modern Firefox versions, will choke on it in wild ways.  Nonetheless,
all you need to do is to put the following snippet in
<i>/etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/30-defaultdepth.conf</i> and restart your session:

<pre>
Section "Screen"
    Identifier    "Default Screen"
    Monitor       "Configured Monitor"
    Device        "Configured Video Device"
    DefaultDepth   30
EndSection
</pre>

<p>X11 additionally does not support HDR at all.

<h3>Linux/BSD + Wayland</h3>
<p>Wayland is still largely experimental and at the edge of usefulness,
though at least bit depth and HDR shouldn't pose a problem.

<p><b>KDE/Plasma:</b> This is the recommended compositor, as you can set up
everything directly through its quirky <i>Display Configuration</i>.
Colour depth appears to favour the deep end automatically.

<p><b>GNOME Shell:</b> For HDR, simply enable it in <i>Displays</i>.
For SDR to work, put the following snippet within <code>&lt;monitor&gt;</code>
sections in <i>~/.config/monitors.xml</i>:

<pre>
&lt;colormode&gt;sdr-native&lt;/colormode&gt;
&lt;maxbpc&gt;10&lt;/maxbpc&gt;
</pre>

<p>Temporary setting for just the colour mode; the <code>-P</code> option should
retain it as well:

<pre>
gdctl set -L -M DP-1 --color-mode sdr-native
</pre>

<p>The compositor&mdash;Mutter&mdash;will ignore anything you've configured
in <i>Colour Management</i>, and it will instead read the monitor's native
profile over the cable from its EDID.  Hopefully, it will be close enough for
sRGB content.  <i>Dawn</i> will read the actual profile from <i>colord</i>.
This is a compromise.

<p><b>Sway/wlroots:</b> For SDR to work, put a snippet similar to the following
in <i>~/.config/sway/config</i>:

<pre>
output DP-1 {
	render_bit_depth 10
	color_profile icc '/usr/share/color/icc/colord/AdobeRGB1998.icc'
}
</pre>

<p>Everything will be limited to sRGB now, but at least nothing will overshoot
it.  Beware that Sway's ICC profile-based colour management is done through
a 33<sup>3</sup> look up table, making it strictly better to keep your monitor
in sRGB if possible.

<h2>Verification</h2>

<p>Open the following test image.  It will exercise your display's true gamut,
and you should also be able to see the finer steps.  The bottom row should look
more like <q>linear 188</q>, not <q>sRGB 128</q> (which indicates scaling in
encoded space).

<p><a href=p3-srgb-ramps.png><img src=p3-srgb-ramps.png
alt="Test image: 10-bit RGB ramps, scaling test" /></a>

<p>Note that <i>Dawn</i> currently implements Bayer dithering for 8-bit
swapchains, which you may need to turn off in settings in order to observe
any difference in those finer steps (i.e. to see the bit depth at all).

<h2>Thumbnails</h2>

<p><i>Dawn</i> produces and stores its own wide-format, colour-managed
thumbnails under:

<ul>
<li><p><b>Linux/BSD:</b> the XDG thumbnail cache:
<i>~/.cache/thumbnails/wide-*</i>
<li><p><b>macOS:</b> <i>~/Library/Caches/thumbnails/wide-*</i>
<li><p><b>Windows:</b> <i>%LOCALAPPDATA%\cache\thumbnails\wide-*</i>
</ul>

<p>These thumbnails will be replaced entirely in a later release.

<h3>Cache Invalidation</h3>

<p>The program slowly accumulates thumbnails of all pictures you see.
Currently, to remove thumbnails for files that can no longer be found,
you need to invoke thumbnail invalidation from the command line:

<ul>
<li>
<p><b>Linux/BSD:</b> you should have Dawn in your system path:
<pre>
dn --invalidate-cache
</pre>
<li>
<p><b>macOS:</b> for the usual location:
<pre>
/Applications/Dawn.app/Contents/MacOS/Dawn --invalidate-cache
</pre>
<li>
<p><b>Windows:</b> for the usual location:
<pre>
"C:\Program Files\@PROJECT_NAME@\dn.exe" --invalidate-cache
</pre>
</ul>

<p>You may also simply delete the directories, which are used by nothing else.

<h2>Lossless JPEG Cropper</h2>

<p>Dawn offers a mode with lossless JPEG cropping and rotating functionality.
It only offers operations which won't cause the image to be reencoded at a loss.
Beware that JPEGs may contain HDR or depth map metadata, which will be stripped.

<p>To control the crop region, use your left and right mouse buttons.
On macOS, the right mouse button can be simulated by holding <kbd>Control</kbd>
while clicking the left mouse button.

<h2>Resources</h2>

<p>The project homepage is at
<a href=@PROJECT_HOMEPAGE_URL@>@PROJECT_HOMEPAGE_URL@</a>.

<script>
var toc = '', all = document.querySelectorAll('h2')
for (var i = 0; i < all.length; i++) {
	var name = all[i].innerHTML.replace(/[^0-9a-z]/ig, '-')
	toc += '<li><p><a href="#' + name + '">' + all[i].innerHTML + '</a></li>'
	all[i].id = name
	all[i].innerHTML = (i + 1) + '. ' + all[i].innerHTML
}
all[0].insertAdjacentHTML('beforebegin',
	'<h2>Table of Contents</h2><ol>' + toc + '</ol>')
</script>
